{
 "about": "Limits: what a run does when a ceiling is reached, for each `when-it-runs-out:` an agent can write. Replayed as reactions.json is (adapters/python/tests/conformance_kit.py), and `expect` has the same fields. A ceiling is reached when the count is AT the figure written, and it is tested between steps: with `tool-calls-at-most: 2` both calls run, and the run goes no further. The first ceiling reached is the one that acts, and `halted` is its word. `answer-with-what-it-has` makes one closing request with no tools offered (the last entry of `offered` is empty) and its text is the answer; that request is told, last in its system text, which ceiling was reached, which calls the model asked for were not made, and to answer from what it has (`closing` in `expect`; `Reached.closing` in pact_adapters/limits.py is the wording, and a call is written as its name, a space, and its arguments as JSON with sorted keys); `stop-and-say-so` ends with a sentence saying which ceiling; `ask-a-person` parks, and a yes gives the run the same budget again from where it is. The first request after the yes, and no other, is told last in its system text that a person said to keep going, what the ceiling it had reached is now, and which calls it asked for were not made (`after-a-yes` in `expect`, one text for each yes; `Reached.raised` is the wording). A no ends the run as `out-of-budget`, and `output` is a sentence naming the ceiling and saying a person was asked and said not to keep going (`Reached.declined` in pact_adapters/limits.py is the wording, for every runtime). When nobody answers in time (`people: [\"nobody\"]`) and the question says `if-nobody-answers: decline`, the run ends the same way and the sentence says nobody answered in time, not that a person said no. The ceiling named is the one that stopped the run: a run at `steps-at-most` and `tool-calls-at-most` together, stopped because a call was not allowed, names `tool-calls-at-most`.",
